# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — ADLC Runbook (manual / local recipe)

How the Principal drives the pipeline by hand. This is the reliable baseline; it needs no
GitHub Actions. Each stage: apply the label → invoke the agent → review the output →
advance. Agents never advance through a gate; you do.

To watch the line while you drive it, run `/adlc-mission-control`: a read-only live page on
your own machine.

The agents you invoke here are this repo's copies of the plugin's, `.claude/agents/adlc-<role>.md`,
written by `/adlc-init`. Each file's `model:` line is the model that agent runs on; `/adlc-model`
prints them all or changes one, and a model named in the call itself still wins for that call.
The Actions lanes run the plugin's own `adlc:<role>` agents, which keep the plugin's defaults.

## Stage 1 — Intake → triage → stories (Product Analyst)

1. Run `/adlc-intake` — from an idea, a PRD, an existing issue, a bug, or a Notion/Linear
   ticket it interviews you for what's missing and files the `stage:intake` issue once you
   confirm. (Already sharp? Create the issue yourself and label it `stage:intake`.)
2. Invoke the **adlc-product-analyst** agent with the issue number (its model is the `model:`
   line in `.claude/agents/adlc-product-analyst.md`). If it stops with numbered questions,
   answer them with `/adlc-intake <issue>` and invoke it again.
3. It **triages** (the `triage` skill), posting an `ADLC-TRIAGE: FAST|FULL` verdict, then either
   the full story set (FULL) or a change brief (FAST), and moves the issue to `gate:stories`.
4. **Gate 1 (you):** accept or return. On accept, apply `stage:design` for the full pipeline —
   **or, if it was triaged FAST and you agree, apply `stage:fast`** (the fast lane below).

## Fast lane — trivial changes (skips design + Gate 1)

For a small, local, non-sensitive change you can skip Stages 2 and 4. Apply `stage:fast`
directly to a fresh issue (your own triage) or after the Analyst recommends FAST.

1. Run the fast-lane cap on your intended scope first — `printf '<files>' | .adlc/scripts/adlc-triage.sh`
   (FULL means it's too big or touches something sensitive → use the full pipeline instead).
2. Invoke the **adlc-builder** agent on the `stage:fast` issue (its model is the `model:` line
   in `.claude/agents/adlc-builder.md`): it makes the scoped change + a test (no ADR — it works
   from the change brief), writes `.adlc/scope/<n>.txt`, and opens a PR.
3. Invoke the **adlc-adversarial-reviewer** agent on the PR (its model is the `model:` line in
   `.claude/agents/adlc-adversarial-reviewer.md`; walk the `adlc:security-gate` classes). The cap
   is enforced again on the real diff — an over-cap/sensitive diff goes to `stage:design`.
4. On a PASS verdict, move the issue to `gate:deploy`
   (`gh issue edit <n> --remove-label stage:fast --add-label gate:deploy` — one state at a
   time, as the fast-lane workflow does). **Gate 2 (you):** the same behind-the-base check
   and merge as the full lane's Gate 2 below. On CHANGES, hand it back to the Builder (the same fix
   cycle as the full lane).

## Stage 2 — Design (Architect, duty 1)

1. Invoke the **adlc-architect** agent on the `stage:design` issue (its model is the `model:`
   line in `.claude/agents/adlc-architect.md`).
2. It writes `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` + a task breakdown comment + blast-radius check, then
   advances to `stage:build`.
3. Review the ADR. (You accept ADRs; the Architect never accepts its own.)

## Stage 3 — Build (Builder)

1. Invoke the **adlc-builder** agent on the `stage:build` issue (its model is the `model:` line
   in `.claude/agents/adlc-builder.md`).
2. It branches `feat/<n>-<slug>`, implements within the declared diff scope (recorded in
   `.adlc/scope/<n>.txt`, which the pre-commit hook enforces), writes AC-traced tests, runs the
   project's test/build commands, and opens a PR.
3. If you run the Builder in an **isolated worktree**, hand it the ADR path so it commits
   the ADR onto its branch (an ADR written only in the main checkout is invisible to the
   branch and gets stranded off the PR).

## Stage 4 — Design-conformance review (Architect, duty 2)

1. Invoke the **adlc-architect** agent on the Builder's PR (its model is the `model:` line in
   `.claude/agents/adlc-architect.md`).
2. It reviews for diff-scope + design conformance and submits `--approve` /
   `--request-changes`. On approval, you (or CI) advance the issue `stage:build → stage:qa`.

## Stage 5 — QA gate (QA & Release/Ops)

1. Invoke the **adlc-qa-release-ops** agent on the `stage:qa` PR (its model is the `model:`
   line in `.claude/agents/adlc-qa-release-ops.md`).
2. It verifies AC coverage, writes missing tests, runs the full battery, and runs the
   **adversarial security gate**. For UI changes it runs browser verification.
3. You run `/security-review` on the PR branch and post findings.
4. On a clean verdict it adds `gate:deploy` and posts a **Proposed Action Card**.

## Gate 2 — Merge + deploy (you)

1. Review the Action Card (its `Base:` line says which commit of the base branch the QA
   battery ran against).
2. Check the PR is not behind the base branch — how many base commits its head lacks:
   `gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/compare/$(gh pr view <n> --json baseRefName,headRefName -q '.baseRefName + "..." + .headRefName')" --jq .behind_by`
   (a head on a fork is `<owner>:<branch>`). On anything but `0`, merge the base branch into
   the PR branch (or ask the Builder to), wait for the checks to go green on the new head, and
   only then merge. Two PRs that are each green on their own head can still make the base
   branch red once both are in, and branch protection's "require branches to be up to date"
   is not available on a private Free-plan repo — this check is the stand-in. Where you merge
   several QA-approved PRs in one sitting, it is the second one that this catches. Do not read
   `gh pr view --json mergeStateStatus` for this: it says `BEHIND` only where that rule is on,
   and a behind PR without it reads `CLEAN` or `UNSTABLE`.
3. Merge the PR (agents can't — the plugin's guard hook blocks `gh pr merge`).
4. Deploy per the `release-ops` skill. Destructive migration? Separate, approved, never
   coupled with the deploy.
5. Tell the QA agent "deploy N happened" for the post-deploy watch.

## Isolated-worktree recipe (optional, for parallel Builder/QA)

Run Builder/QA in a `git worktree` off the branch so their edits don't collide with your
main checkout. Wire the agent's `cd` into the worktree in its first Bash call; verify
against the **serving checkout** (the worktree), not a stale main.
